#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167843} :activity/id #uuid "be95ee71-ebc4-546c-a738-6a6db824342c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167997 :tutorial/content "Consider a *decoupled* linear system of ODEs\n![](images/3f/3f7a114fa75100f8cd3ccf72822758e668f3a6a0824ad5f5437ebef138928fb5.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we consider only decoupled systems with nonzero eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8ed089264b88bdcf4018f44db6dade448063b56a6ded558fb74d7c2ca61c0de.svg) so the origin is the equilibrium point we will classify.\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues for a diagonal matrix are its diagonal entries, ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) \nThe corresponding eigenvectors are parallel to the coordinate axes:\n![](images/83/8337ebfc4aae4b8e97438cdba58872082bdc8965932659fdf2f8ff9cf061b375.svg)\n\nSo, the general solution of the system is given by\n![](images/fc/fcb5c1cea49dfe7aa1058f64d859a8b47c363f7f6195bf68056b18f1fa6d37f2.svg)\n\nNotice that if a solution starts on one of the coordinate axes, it remains on that axis. These are called **straight-line solutions**:\n\n- On the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis (![](images/8d/8da7ef7a61d4c5af4d28575fcd263714cf3bda8e7d94985bd4bea5c03eb2d53f.svg)): \n![](images/aa/aa9cd3c49609ee6d09a61c9c31438e4297620feedfda1073fbc55c08e7c53d01.svg)\n\n- On the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis (![](images/f2/f271ee76900762067d886b30871a9ea7538ae69318ad4514e5b39cfdce12644b.svg)):\n![](images/50/509154a662b215d445b282396a8f43828e582d43282467a0da73843ade7ec5d8.svg)\n\nThe stability of these solutions as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the *signs* of the eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) We will explore the three main stability cases in the upcoming examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "3ab2b715-abcc-511c-bbda-a2664d39efda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14436 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a60bbebf-7d50-57b0-b823-f5cde68f6ecb" :step/math-academy-id 588112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168002 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0.$ \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/75/75e7a0e16141db2a8da210c1ab30fc7328ccc98f861afec4db69d951ff3c9121.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/28/280e99a308962d43d1060be7100e294623d3bc3bfbe0579ab61d817695ad9e45.svg) Since $\\lambda_2>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity. \n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "302544e8-9eeb-5087-ae71-7143ffcbad9f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14460 :tutorial/title "Both Positive Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "752fcd7d-e772-51d0-84f5-e0724d157464" :step/math-academy-id 588113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168003 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n![](images/3e/3e1a6630e46ba96ca21946d9feedc452d8416fe5bac17d5e8275c41c489d19ea.svg)\nwhere ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg)\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/a1/a12ab1d3c69a589aa8de66fe8d14ed19b2938a9ef5591d87860e101a95afdc55.svg) Since ![](images/3d/3db8d2103adafd2be29df482295b07ef63576be6f152793463e034e808725edf.svg) we have that ![](images/bb/bb6223675110a379d33203ff637afaa6aefd3edf507b5a93386c608aa7aafca5.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/aa/aae0f8015e0a575ca1e1fdbb37d011cbf78574649e3c9874163c3f14f1ca9929.svg) Since ![](images/23/23d0a8291d49cc1d2f0c6cff735a4d5214c2d9079dca67d803846af9b063358f.svg) we have that ![](images/69/69c464920e94e968ac5b2fe07f52d5e8a5e694f0fab6c9bfdcfd1b359a82e89e.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/11/119ac25f56cfb326a9eb39eaf63c41e8497147c80b14da3a38e557e8aeb4f905.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "98c957b9-817c-5bdd-9e7a-9680641d5c55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14461 :tutorial/title "Both Negative Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "4ed4db19-c2d0-5c82-a414-637905d1a91d" :step/math-academy-id 588114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166536 :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420022}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"} :step/id #uuid "0849e9c4-6231-5416-bf0c-d15d2ba66f0e" :step/math-academy-id 588115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166537 :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525064}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"} :step/id #uuid "d06d3f5b-d8b3-5195-a3ad-20d231c0a7e8" :step/math-academy-id 588118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168001 :tutorial/content "For a decoupled system\n![](images/df/df904ec5214399987c94f99323d3c3c4c06d9e19242c0d580574488011475acc.svg)\nwe know the *signs* of ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) tell us the type of the equilibrium (sink/source/saddle). But how can we determine the shape of trajectories in cases when the origin is a source (![](images/bb/bb6302eab08feb0614c62d4ea8012676675715a469dd30a7321ec228ff7ef4e9.svg)) or a sink (![](images/be/be8860e40a1c229262d3bf3375732f4b4583cad7aaf63f3a17e871bbd5a51abb.svg))?\n\nTo do this, we can compare the rates of change of ![](images/f7/f7dbfa03976240534a1b57f2cc59c474401a0d9524bc5f627736da01a0d775c1.svg) and ![](images/cc/cc436bde605f3822acb66d36528ba42a812a50fdbea361b31f0dbd0cba61d810.svg) by looking at the slope of the tangent line to the trajectory, ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg) Assuming ![](images/37/371aec72d3e326e3e52fbd1a1cfc06be06b33e3054e60b8c286a9fcbac39141e.svg) we have:\n![](images/be/be21baf0c518d85b105b0f569382fbc47075c268847437046b042db36a9b4b8a.svg)\n\nAs we can see, the long-term behavior of the slope as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the sign of the exponent, ![](images/7c/7cde6653feb562f0d136150bffa6f2925af0c7dbcb9ede5c7f1b3e0f10f3796d.svg)\n\nThis comparison is governed by which eigenvalue is larger. We call the larger eigenvalue (the one that is more positive, or less negative) the **dominant eigenvalue**.\n\nIn the next slides, we will analyze the source and sink cases separately." :tutorial/id #uuid "406ac21a-bfe3-5af2-bc34-8bb09ae11870" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14456 :tutorial/title "Dominant Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "7d22549d-343c-5238-823d-0fdd3d6b11cf" :step/math-academy-id 588121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "edbdd357-26e5-5671-9724-72c90fe9512d" :step/math-academy-id 588122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168004 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n![](images/e1/e1efdd0b8c037c01450042384ce3bac663d0b549fa9557d0db09b692754cab0f.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fd81402-072b-5676-9cac-f53af20f3174" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14462 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "b8abcadd-58fc-5f3e-a1a9-f6f32aaa7405" :step/math-academy-id 588125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166540 :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444147}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "6cadc020-1f0c-5e3d-88b7-c3977e63cc74" :step/math-academy-id 588126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168020 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$ (the opposite case, where $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2>0,$ is similar). \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/4d/4dc3aaf0f7dfd85070df84c13abe2cf02d85a704167569b3d3a76f2b58178c5b.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin. This is called an **unstable axis**.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/76/76f6b9bbcdff2c87295fb0cd14dbdbbaa5e6c75eeb49c1cc158a4e8bd3da059d.svg) Since $\\lambda_2<0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin. This is called a **stable axis**.\n\nBecause one axis is stable and the other is unstable, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **saddle point**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), while\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory bends from the stable direction and then peels away along the unstable direction.\n\n![](images/f2/f2538a59050e604d85fd9726e688cfbd2a5c9bdff4d15dd5ed465ed04f7290b4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8fe29b2e-2a4a-5a2d-aaea-49ed3c5a7c1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14565 :tutorial/title "One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "7cfc4842-67bf-5d76-b1c9-2607d80a8a6e" :step/math-academy-id 588184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168057 :tutorial/content "Let's analyze the case where the origin is a source, with ![](images/b8/b89d2dea375e40cafca8a025742ea1ba94b01ff3dd5926dc21b62444847e237f.svg)  For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories move away from the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/3c/3cda8c604691cfc8889d64007bce911c2ea6bd3ba06a7a14cb3adef0cacbccfc.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) In this case:\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/a5/a5bd23277d5f3a652a992839592d09551cd2278773b0f92cbdc06b247f69dc64.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) In this case:\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move away from the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/28/286b72736b47e9d318f585fc0ef0ae69e4150914c3ca7a8981d236d08305a6a5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "534a4770-a0a6-5b07-a36e-0bc08f985630" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14895 :tutorial/title "Unstable Node (Source)"} :step/id #uuid "ee8907e9-ae68-5313-b486-9d33968f5dc9" :step/math-academy-id 608071 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168058 :tutorial/content "Now, let's analyze the case where the origin is a sink, with ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg) For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/48/481eb665d12add01a80a4646ec2762c89c9a543bf87b959b246d469a450eca6d.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as trajectories approach the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/80/80105ab4e61c0e2a59d11da4b534753b3c811b424f1e0b82704c6e28faecf9b8.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as trajectories approach the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move toward the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/b6/b665d5328ae362ada2208da2583fd90247078490145426072455d43f8d36227f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b95d431-e3ab-5635-b641-a019d23397b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14896 :tutorial/title "Stable Node (Sink)"} :step/id #uuid "78ab8590-2a54-5795-87c0-331930a7d959" :step/math-academy-id 608072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167849}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167013}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444153} :activity/id #uuid "9a145625-7556-5712-927a-f0c028bebc85" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af9e8ee-427e-5b4a-9c20-64d8c764d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444123} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfdedb2-5206-5bb7-bc0c-b4380ef84ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444126} {:answer/id #uuid "6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444128} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7adf14-40d3-5a8b-b017-a61006c8a570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444135} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fef36d-c8ab-5030-a6ef-407316ba8a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444128} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c351da-96ee-57a7-ad19-0d1fa7a32b4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05eed77e-6ddf-5b27-b62c-35f5a73a8482" :question/math-academy-id "q-332692" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aaeba63f6237121fe226eb30e5378d3e93129bb2782b9c8acb8d73ac719676b3.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f0eb2d5-220a-51b8-8ecc-e34f70d2ec6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6095321f-3c3a-5564-bd76-8ac789465110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/63/635e8687fbccc7612f692ec1a1abaa9802bafe9c3773e4b0c058a18813e7249e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391146} {:answer/id #uuid "9f24dea2-7b89-5ce9-93de-ca7b97049c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a5/a5ae215022638430d0c89dddd97c01ae7598b6251bd013ef48ca9771029b8cd6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391147} {:answer/id #uuid "f70a8ce1-5b6e-5473-90f3-bf87bae67685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c9/c9816edf7fb99b759361abc4333f3ed0d3676d2c77db2210367466b66837bca8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391148} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcceae6-4fa4-5965-9386-71fc659c0936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391149} {:answer/id #uuid "65324022-a078-5bdb-b4aa-e0381dbb7e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4f/4f613e414cdb619a9b09f25a47b7e4cad953440c40d91f876322d7e1cb59b8c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391149} :answer-field/id #uuid "41bd2e0d-7517-5053-bcbd-08e7df5307b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333177" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/99/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)"} :step/id #uuid "54129fc6-c6e7-58f1-988d-b38903f3b41b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59d44033-2953-53f5-9485-d986fa666d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444127} {:answer/id #uuid "73a7ffd1-1cf0-5378-bc11-62171a0c3abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444131} :answer-field/id #uuid "62075cea-c86f-5ede-af51-3b9b6e1b7a2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a24a7424-a635-59c1-b0a8-3d8d09c20d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444134} {:answer/id #uuid "d97d6664-8e4c-536c-b723-d007c6be0edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444138} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02dcf68-1aa6-50e4-bcf6-a43a1c61f609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7975e3-2735-589b-a5cb-e7baa70e5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444120} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8766a2-b885-5372-9ea9-597fde6b723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444121} {:answer/id #uuid "40931ed8-a2d3-5a7d-af42-4ceeceafa5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7391bdf-0a46-510a-b0ad-0b46b06955a6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cc95b14-28b1-5770-bd6e-4ea958da90bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333222" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/665ff96d191a9b2dc6dc57f1c3a97743dcb1a26c4118151716bdf430a4c014f3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "aed772c0-58fb-5fcf-9ea0-9de0620f174d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444124} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444130} {:answer/id #uuid "85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444132} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} :answer-field/id #uuid "540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332687" :question/problem "![](images/39/390321da0e4c3367d121a1561ccc41ca4960c912ff53d909acd1c90f505758ce.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9e90e37-14ef-58a0-a386-45d0aaa27b85" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461d44-24c5-575d-be64-3d0e46a32765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7a/7aaaf92ef853dcc88c787120fbb380a1529a49504255fb1b05803e3712587c90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444122} {:answer/id #uuid "62879d01-d1d9-55e2-ab3d-537c10ca44a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/19/196d1a61793b619bf86f3ed18852f819cd27821e0ad0388bcd05621e60ba46ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444129} {:answer/id #uuid "c46723d7-b8d3-5ade-85f9-0bf37a1a2dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f1162ad9109f4117ea94321ee1e2192734b3a895b35232a0788c0ff754e2bade.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444136} {:answer/id #uuid "ce200fd8-672b-5314-9ac6-4d8b99ea280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444137} {:answer/id #uuid "dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444139} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfc6238d-540c-5d03-81af-c08e27ebdfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97c6c7fa-18db-5e5d-84d1-3076bd6f5c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-333183" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2c81ff3c50013c149dc6a5db67bbe11cccdbbef22ce2c0712101f4a358bcb8c.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{2t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{2t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{t})^{′}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}}{{c}_{1}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "dbe1ae80-d585-551e-b83d-d3f42e88a7bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444152}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444119}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525079} :activity/id #uuid "8170d687-2150-5ce0-9fbc-f1d2e19d49be" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c87346-c0ee-53c3-94ac-95b49a1375c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302693} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1993f9-8087-5c6b-a1ad-dedd2a035b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303328} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e742a2-b236-57f3-8979-a3b9a473fc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303672} {:answer/id #uuid "d10f1558-a4b7-543d-abeb-1abd68004cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303745} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1b612c-1d62-5b79-aa3b-7cf7a714c584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303672} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe78fafd-1fb0-5c2f-9ba1-e3c0b222dbf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd958f9f-ce7e-5925-b2cc-b90dad87e64d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332710" :question/problem "![](images/83/83a565accc834d7c16091de22eb43ba80c0ce9c4290f567d1e0ccc009beca565.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1f042b9b-5541-5e4f-946e-597ca617d6fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461def2d-f65b-5702-81dc-9c688ad9792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391171} {:answer/id #uuid "64590024-0b5d-5509-b3ed-cec5555ef505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391172} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097811-7f04-5636-ac94-08d654db1f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391171} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78979b2-0930-5c53-983d-999f23a46873" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5edfc04a-0185-57d1-a0a8-90f14d6bb9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391175} {:answer/id #uuid "83c65d7a-dd3e-5392-bb3e-959427004b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391175} :answer-field/id #uuid "110033c6-9468-57ae-9c4b-2409f3360843" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad3d999-893c-5b32-86ed-3c76b9777309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391178} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf150cb-9ddf-51fd-a897-b57d640d5664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391178} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb6a2c1-2961-5d3d-8a0c-af139a57aa82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7685d561-af7c-5963-8ff0-5f1c98ad02e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333227" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b4/b406c864e820819c5ae6f548c7a025a1e1d8197fb785ea5689080034e04210cb.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7590c9da-e337-57de-aa4b-d285843f0aa4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a058e89-acfe-528d-ab94-fd31f2e16a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e7/e7a9385b508c7bb1591501fd8bc830faa78ebc3a2f2ad8279ecfe88328ce8bbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305392} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7afe2f-b74d-5903-8e22-e33beb75077b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b829471223fd7bc2bd7a6c1183729f6eda34260822edc7c9cb8b6e86a7a55e6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305556} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb7e27-61e6-5dd3-ab55-ab3de3ab406b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a7/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305638} {:answer/id #uuid "450a922a-3f5c-5a09-9e71-6f2f4a6bad64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305829} {:answer/id #uuid "ed66b242-caca-5caf-a774-faa25d71628d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/71c58730ea546deee365729cab6f1cae3f9aa73409a302067dbbf91fdd85a24d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305638} :answer-field/id #uuid "bded1a3e-f39b-5a62-b510-7b2710d4baad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44b4a9a2-6730-57eb-96aa-0793a3571f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-333192" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/b1/b1efa82f75d22a304e29adc5a791086bb9d9bf43b2f5c97ba8563f43fd13cc4a.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/a7/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png)"} :step/id #uuid "8198ac8e-1e9d-5686-bcf7-2cb7faeeb3bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8716e607-f4af-5764-b0f4-37fea7ca8f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164470} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c26e-b8e1-50ac-8f1c-7401387c76b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a4/a42df9a876ef99a68ecb211a691211f7398c71d32426c55ce906171f1e563692.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164471} {:answer/id #uuid "a70dc086-4d01-5f96-9237-82d92d5496e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2a8375ddd5ab11a79bda95a0f1dc51daf62bc452a3ffec8347227f069bc7bd57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164472} {:answer/id #uuid "b60a1882-b0f8-5e3d-9e59-9e94e2f5bd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2b/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164473} {:answer/id #uuid "5b93229b-5f6f-571e-b668-8e3e26328d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e4bad1b9f0d5b0621fdbd7caf5278018ccbff5a60ede579f591ffbeacd7f741c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186164471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7252f43-4dd1-594c-840b-b764aff0a2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39b2d4dd-f57e-54ad-a67c-f1f62018e20b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333187" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2196ea04e12f3c706300e0c9d15806606e408f5e582d3e4f2b9c62f0efc343f.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/14/1429eda4336d47aec06c7dd673ff6163203423739c520fd0c83db1c5cb072ac7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "a8502bd6-d620-5db8-94f8-350537638008" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff6638-43e0-5544-89de-9608b0711546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524397} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6a586f-0cf2-5116-a740-1a9d8f1242c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524508} {:answer/id #uuid "88a6fa17-dfc1-5da9-b77a-35d12299dbb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524596} {:answer/id #uuid "c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524709} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2ca45a-40c5-5ab4-aa0e-968f3478e0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524709} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d768191-43c2-52ef-8bd4-abb5c4c7e134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332719" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cd11c63564050d5851c53e6a808e6a7b73ee33ce6cbe77c9278c6a3429eaec5.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ae3c91ec-0646-58d2-a00b-c0e68120022f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525123}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524368}]}
